diagnosis_text:
A complete multi-layer investigation of the `ospf_enterprise_static` lab found no fault at any layer that could explain the reported timeout symptoms on `host_1_1_1_1`.

**Affected component:** None identified — all subsystems are healthy.

**Mechanism:** The user reported that all connections to internal servers time out. To confirm or rule this out, every network layer was tested in sequence. At L2, `infra_sweep` and `l2_snapshot` found no ACL/firewall drop rules, no addressing anomalies, no duplicate MACs, and all hosts hold valid IPs with default routes. At the routing layer, `ospf_snapshot` confirmed all six FRR routers (router_core_1/2/3, switch_dist_1_1/2_1, switch_server_access) are fully converged with complete OSPF adjacencies (all Full state) and 10 OSPF routes each; `tc_snapshot` found no bandwidth shaping or packet corruption qdiscs anywhere. At the service layer, `service_snapshot` showed `host_1_1_1_1` correctly resolving web0–3.local via 10.200.0.2 (correct answers) and successfully reaching all four web servers with HTTP 200 responses at ~4 ms. `pressure_sweep` found no stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket exhaustion on any device.

**Localization:** None — no device is implicated.

**Root cause:** The reported fault is unconfirmed: the network is fully functional at the time of investigation with no mechanism or historical trace of a disruption.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the user experiences the issue again, capturing the exact error message and timestamp will help distinguish a transient external event from a recurring lab fault.
=== END ===